# Line-coverage minimums, enforced by `task cover:gate` and by CI.
#
# Gate the logic that decides what a collector reports, not the glue around
# it. A number on an HTTP adapter makes people write tests for the wrong thing.
#
# Format: <module-relative-package-path> <minimum-percent>. Subpackages are
# included. Paths are module-relative so that the module path lives in exactly
# one place outside go.mod files.
#
# Generated code (*.pb.go, *.gen.go, zz_generated.*) never counts: coverage on
# it measures the generator, not us.
#
# A gate whose packages have no statements yet passes with a printed notice.
# A gate pointing at a directory that does not exist is an error, so renaming
# a package cannot quietly switch its gate off.

# The contract's semantic rules. Everything protobuf cannot express is here,
# and the SDK, the host and the conformance suite all rely on this one
# implementation being right.
api/collector/v1   90

# What a plugin author writes against. A mistake here is a mistake in every
# collector anyone ever writes, ours and theirs.
sdk/go/collector   90

# The suite a third party runs. A check that does not catch what it claims to
# catch is worse than no check, so its own tests drive it with a collector
# that lies in one specific way each time.
sdk/conformance    85

# The Keycloak collector's resolution logic. A bug here produces a grant that
# looks right and is not, which is the expensive kind: nothing downstream can
# tell that "alice has admin" was derived wrongly.
plugins/keycloak/internal/collect 85
plugins/keycloak/internal/admin   75

# The GitHub collector. Its resolution is where the four routes to a
# repository come together, and a mistake there produces a grant that looks
# right and is not, the same class of bug as the Keycloak collector's, in a
# source that states less of the answer itself.
plugins/github/internal/collect 80
plugins/github/internal/api     80

# The AWS collector. Its mapping is where a role becomes both a principal and
# an entitlement, and where AWS's sentinels have to stay distinguishable from
# a definite answer: read "no information" as "never used" and the account
# root reports as dormant.
#
# The iam package is lower because most of it is the AWS SDK adapter, which
# cannot be exercised without an account. What is gated there is the parsing
# with real logic in it: trust policies and the credential report.
plugins/awsiam/internal/collect 85
plugins/awsiam/internal/iam     50

# The Entra collector. Its collection is where a role held through a group
# becomes a derived grant, and where Graph's silences (an id-only member, a
# blank sign-in, a missing property) have to stay distinguishable from a
# definite answer: read a missing accountEnabled as "disabled" and every grant
# the user holds is skipped in review.
#
# The graph package is the client. It is exercised against a fake Graph that
# issues real tokens and verifies real client assertions, so what is not
# covered is what only a live tenant can answer, and it is listed in the
# collector's README.
plugins/entra/internal/collect 85
plugins/entra/internal/graph   80
